# Line endings are a build input, not a preference.
#
# Without this file a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites every text file to CRLF, and
# the same commit then produces a .vsix whose members differ byte-for-byte from the one built on
# Linux — identical content, different checksum, and an artifact hash that cannot be quoted as proof
# of what a release contains. It also hands the verification gate two different renderings of the
# same source, which is where a line-ending-dependent pattern goes unnoticed until a runner leg
# reports a security setting as missing.
#
# eol=lf wins over a developer's core.autocrlf, so the working tree matches the blob everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files whose bytes are not text. Normalising these would corrupt them silently.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.vsix binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary

# Extension metadata and assets that must stay exact.
*.json text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf
